(function(){'use strict';var lib=window.JASPER_FANFIC_DATA;if(!lib||!Array.isArray(lib.series))return;var edits={"su-14":{"number":14,"title":"The Long Way Back","subtitle":"Stars Between the Strings · Chapter 14 of 40","content":"The road back from the music festival took twice as long because Greg refused to admit the van was lost.\n\n“I’m not lost,” he said for the fourth time.\n\n“You just asked a cow for directions.”\n\n“The cow was near a sign.”\n\n“The cow was eating grass.”\n\nThe cow wasn't the only one inconvenienced by our navigation. At the next crossroads Greg pulled into a small service station, where a woman was sorting cold drinks into a refrigerator that clearly needed repairing.\n\n“Hey,” he said, leaning in through the doorway. “Any chance the coastal highway's this way?”\n\nShe looked out at the van, at his hand-drawn directions and at the embarrassed grin he wore whenever he knew he'd made something worse.\n\n“The road you're on goes back to the quarry,” she said. “Where did you get that map?”\n\nGreg unfolded it. The paper crackled.\n\nShe laughed. “Oh, honey. That bridge was washed out six years ago.”\n\n“I've been driving on optimism and expired cartography.”\n\n“Mostly expired cartography.” She drew a route on the back of a receipt, then added a warning about the gravel turn after the old mill.\n\n“Want me to buy something so this isn't a free consultation?” he asked.\n\n“You can buy something because you're thirsty,” she said. “Directions are still free.”\n\nI liked her immediately. We bought two bottles of water and a packet of crackers because we actually needed them. Greg stood at the counter wondering whether to tip for the map. She told him the jar was for the local fire company and that the donation was voluntary. He put in a little cash without making a show of it.\n\nBack in the van, he fixed the receipt to the dashboard with a bit of tape.\n\n“You know, I could've figured that out,” he said.\n\n“You asked a cow first.”\n\n“A very promising cow.”\n\nI snorted and reached across the bench seat. He slid his hand into mine without looking away from the road.\n\n“I like this better,” he admitted.\n\n“Being lost?”\n\n“Being able to ask when I am.”\n\nHe squeezed my fingers. Then he missed the turn by about thirty feet, pulled over safely, and looked at me with such miserable comic timing that I started laughing before he could speak.\n\n“It had a thoughtful expression.”\n\nI held the paper map on my lap and tried not to laugh. The festival had ended beneath a pink evening sky. Lila had played a brilliant set. Greg had introduced me to old friends, new musicians, and one man who had made a three-hour argument about harmonicas sound like a spiritual experience.\n\nI had danced with Greg in the grass. I had eaten fried dough. I had listened to him play a new song and felt my whole body recognize the moment before the first verse ended.\n\nNow the van's engine made a worrying noise.\n\n“It sounds like a drawer full of rocks,” I said.\n\n“That’s normal.”\n\n“It is not.”\n\n“It’s a vintage sound.”\n\n“The van is trying to die.”\n\n“It has a flair for drama.”\n\nThe engine coughed. Greg guided the van onto the shoulder. A sign announced that the nearest town was twelve miles away.\n\nHe turned off the ignition.\n\nSilence filled the cabin.\n\n“I’m going to say something,” I began.\n\n“Please don’t say the van is dead.”\n\n“The van is dead.”\n\nGreg rested his forehead against the steering wheel.\n\n“You said it first,” I added.\n\n“I know.”\n\nThe sun lowered toward the fields. Cars passed occasionally, their headlights flashing across the windows. Greg opened the hood and stared into the engine compartment with the confidence of a man who had learned just enough about machines to become dangerous.\n\n“Can you fix it?” I asked.\n\n“Maybe.”\n\n“That word is not mechanically reassuring.”\n\nHe found a loose belt, a cracked hose, and a tool he swore had not been in the van when they left. Nothing made the engine willing to restart.\n\n“We’ll call for help,” he said.\n\nHe tried his phone. No signal.\n\nI tried mine. One bar appeared, then vanished.\n\nThe familiar pressure began in my chest.\n\nGreg saw it. “Hey.”\n\n“I’m fine.”\n\n“I didn’t ask.”\n\nI leaned against the van. “We’re stranded.”\n\n“Temporarily.”\n\n“You don’t know that.”\n\n“No. But I know we have water, food, blankets, and two phones that will eventually find civilization.”\n\n“What if they don’t?”\n\n“Then we become legends of the cow road.”\n\nI tried to laugh. It came out wrong.\n\nGreg wiped his hands on a rag and came to stand in front of me. “Do you want a plan?”\n\n“Yes.”\n\n“We wait until the next car passes, then flag it down if it’s safe. If it doesn’t, we walk toward the town while staying on the shoulder. We keep the lights on until the battery says otherwise. We eat the sandwiches before they become science.”\n\n“You have a plan.”\n\n“I have several.”\n\n“Why didn’t you say that?”\n\n“You were looking at the engine like you wanted it to apologize.”\n\n“It should.”\n\n“I agree.”\n\nThe next car stopped. The driver called a tow service and offered them a ride to the town. Greg stayed close to me the entire time, his hand resting lightly against my back whenever the road became uneven.\n\nAt the small motel, the clerk gave me a room with two beds and a window facing a field. The tow truck would come in the morning.\n\n“We can take the separate beds,” Greg said.\n\nI looked at him. “Do you want to?”\n\n“No.”\n\n“Then don’t.”\n\nHe smiled, but the nervousness did not leave his eyes.\n\nThe room was clean, plain, and too quiet after the festival. I showered first. When I returned, Greg sat on one bed with a paper cup of motel coffee.\n\n“Your turn,” I said.\n\n“I’m afraid the shower will judge me.”\n\n“The cow already did that.”\n\nHe disappeared into the bathroom. The water turned on. I opened the curtains, then closed them when a moth threw itself against the glass.\n\nWhen Greg came out, his hair was damp and his shirt clung to his shoulders. I looked away too late.\n\n“You’re staring,” he said.\n\n“You’re wet.”\n\n“That sounds like an observation.”\n\n“It is.”\n\nHe sat beside me. “Are you okay?”\n\n“I’m tired.”\n\n“That’s true.”\n\n“And embarrassed.”\n\n“Why?”\n\n“Because the van broke down and I immediately panicked.”\n\n“The van broke down. Panicking was a reasonable response.”\n\n“You made a plan.”\n\n“I panicked too. I just did it under the hood.”\n\nI turned toward him. “You did?”\n\n“I have no idea what half those parts do.”\n\n“You looked confident.”\n\n“I had a rag.”\n\nThe laughter came easier this time. Greg leaned back against the headboard.\n\n“I used to think I had to be the person who knew what to do,” he said. “Especially when I was younger. If I was funny enough, useful enough, calm enough, maybe nobody would notice I was terrified.”\n\n“I notice.”\n\n“I know.”\n\n“Does that bother you?”\n\n“No.”\n\n“Why not?”\n\n“Because you don’t use it against me.”\n\nThe words made me look at him.\n\nGreg reached for my hand. “There’s a difference between being seen and being exposed.”\n\nI turned my palm upward. He laced his fingers through mine.\n\n“I want to be seen by you,” he said. “Even when I’m not impressive.”\n\n“You asked a cow for directions.”\n\n“Exactly.”\n\nI kissed him. The motel room softened around the edges. Greg's hand rose to my cheek, then moved to the back of my neck. He held me close, warm and familiar.\n\nThe bed creaked when I shifted. Greg pulled back just enough to look at me.\n\n“Still okay?”\n\n“Yes.”\n\n“Do you want—”\n\n“I want you to stop asking questions for thirty seconds.”\n\nHe smiled. “I can try.”\n\n“Try now.”\n\nHe kissed me again.\n\nThe motel room filled with the quiet sounds of affection. We talked until we were too tired to keep our eyes open and fell asleep with the television muttering softly in the background.\n\nIn the morning, the tow truck arrived. The van was repaired with a part the mechanic described as “mostly compatible.” Greg paid, thanked him, and promised not to ask livestock for directions again.\n\nOn the road home, my hand rested in his.\n\n“The trip was good,” I said.\n\n“Even the breakdown?”\n\n“Especially the breakdown.”\n\nGreg glanced at me. “You’re developing an alarming appreciation for chaos.”\n\n“I’m developing an appreciation for what you do when chaos arrives.”\n\nHis thumb brushed over my knuckles.\n\nThe van carried me toward Beach City, toward the bakery and the boardwalk and the couch with a blanket waiting on it.\n\nIt had broken down on the way there.\n\nIt was still carrying me home.\n\nThe scenic route passed through a town with a clock tower, three antique shops, and a bakery that advertised pies in the window. Greg pulled over before I could ask.\n\n“This is not our route,” I said.\n\n“The route has become more interesting.”\n\n“The route is a road.”\n\n“Roads have personalities.”\n\n“You and the van are both impossible.”\n\nHe bought a pie. The baker asked whether I was newlyweds. Greg choked on a laugh. I answered that I was simply trying to survive a road trip.\n\n“That was a missed opportunity,” Greg said once I was outside.\n\n“For what?”\n\n“A very specific joke.”\n\n“You can tell it to the cow.”\n\n“The cow would understand.”\n\nI ate pie on a bench beneath the clock tower. The town moved around me slowly: a dog pulling at its leash, an old couple arguing about directions, a teenager carrying a stack of books.\n\n“Do you ever think about living somewhere like this?” I asked.\n\n“Sometimes.”\n\n“A small town. A little house. A kitchen that doesn’t move.”\n\n“Sometimes.”\n\n“Would you?”\n\nGreg looked at the pie in his hands. “I could. I’m not sure I would be happy if I did it because someone else thought it would make me easier to love.”\n\nI nodded. “I understand that.”\n\n“I could also keep the van and visit.”\n\n“You’d park it in the driveway.”\n\n“It would be a guest house.”\n\n“It has delusions again.”\n\n“It has aspirations.”\n\nThe conversation did not resolve where either of us would live. It made room for the possibility that home could take different shapes over time.\n\nWhen I reached Beach City that evening, Greg parked outside my apartment instead of his.\n\n“Do you want to come up?” I asked.\n\n“Yes.”\n\n“You sound tired.”\n\n“I’m tired and I want you.”\n\nThe directness made me smile.\n\nInside, I played music quietly and shared the last of the pie. Greg washed the plates while I unpacked the blankets.\n\n“The van survived,” he said.\n\n“Barely.”\n\n“A lot like us.”\n\nI crossed the room and kissed him. “We’re doing better than barely.”\n\nHe held me close. “We are.”\n\nThe night unfolded with fewer jokes and more honesty. We talked until sleep became unavoidable, then held each other in the quiet afterward.\n\nIn the morning, Greg left a slice of pie beside my coffee and called it a road-trip blessing.\n\nWe ate it together.\n\nSlowly.","word_count":1842},"su-17":{"number":17,"title":"The Almost Goodbye","subtitle":"Stars Between the Strings · Chapter 17 of 40","content":"The almost goodbye arrived in an email with a subject line that said: WE WOULD LIKE TO OFFER YOU THE POSITION.\n\nI read it three times before telling Greg.\n\nThe job was in another city. It paid more than the community center. It came with a stable schedule, health benefits, and a small apartment included for the first six months. It was the sort of opportunity people were supposed to be grateful for.\n\nI sat at the kitchen table with my laptop open. Greg stood at the counter, slicing an apple into uneven pieces.\n\n“That’s good,” he said.\n\n“Yes.”\n\n“It sounds good.”\n\n“It does.”\n\nHe finished cutting the apple and set the knife down.\n\n“When do they need an answer?”\n\n“Friday.”\n\nGreg missed the compost container with an apple core. He stared at it on the floor, then at me.\n\n\"Well, that's a metaphor I'm refusing to take personally.\"\n\nI laughed in spite of myself. He picked it up and dropped it where it belonged before sitting at the table.\n\n\"Does the offer say who you'd be working with? Real people, I mean, not the corporate version where everyone is a passionate family.\"\n\nI scrolled through the attachment. There was a salary, but no staffing plan. There was an apartment, but no explanation of whether I could keep the lease if the position ended.\n\n\"Huh,\" he said. \"I played enough gigs for exposure to know a nice-sounding offer can leave out the expensive parts.\"\n\n\"You are not negotiating my career for me.\"\n\n\"Buddy, I can barely negotiate with the van. I just want you to get the whole song before you agree to play.\"\n\nHe slid the pencil across the table. I made a list of questions about hours, sick leave, staff support, and housing. The email took four minutes to write. The choice didn't become easy, but at least it was my choice and not a guess.\n\n\"If I move,\" I said, \"I don't stop being ethically non-monogamous. I prefer polyamory. I still need room to be honest with the people I love, including you.\"\n\nGreg nodded. \"Yeah. We've talked about that. Distance doesn't turn honesty into a special occasion.\"\n\nHe glanced at the screen again. \"Ask about parking, too.\"\n\n\"For you?\"\n\n\"For a hypothetical retired rock star whose van is a work of art.\"\n\nI took his hand under the table and kissed his knuckles. He grinned, a little shy beneath the joke.\n\nThe deadline remained Friday. We had a few more questions, and I had somebody beside me who wasn't trying to answer them in my place.\n\n“That’s soon.”\n\n“Yes.”\n\nGreg sat across from me. He looked at the email, then at my face.\n\n“Do you want it?”\n\n“I don’t know.”\n\n“That’s okay.”\n\n“It would be good for me.”\n\n“Good is not the same as wanted.”\n\n“You always say things like that when I’m trying to be practical.”\n\n“Practical is important. It just shouldn’t be the only voice in the room.”\n\nI closed the laptop. “What if I take it?”\n\nGreg's fingers curled around the edge of the table.\n\n“Then we figure out what that means.”\n\n“You would do long distance?”\n\n“I would try.”\n\n“You might hate it.”\n\n“I might.”\n\n“You might meet someone else.”\n\n“I might.”\n\nI looked away.\n\nGreg reached across the table but stopped before touching me. “I’m not going to tell you not to take the job.”\n\n“Would you want me to stay?”\n\n“Yes.”\n\nThe word hurt.\n\n“Then why aren’t you asking me to?”\n\n“Because I want you to stay because you want this life, not because I made leaving painful.”\n\n“You make everything sound simple.”\n\n“It isn’t simple. It’s just clear.”\n\nThe email waited behind the closed laptop.\n\nFor three days, I tried to decide. I went to work. I answered questions. I played music with Greg. I slept beside him and woke before he did, watching dawn move across his face.\n\nEverywhere, the choice followed.\n\nAt the community center, Mara found me staring at a box of drumsticks.\n\n“You’re leaving,” she said.\n\nI blinked. “I haven’t decided.”\n\n“That wasn’t what I asked.”\n\n“I don’t know.”\n\n“You’re using Greg's word now.”\n\n“He doesn’t own it.”\n\n“Neither do you.”\n\nI laughed despite myself.\n\nMara leaned against the supply cabinet. “Do you want the job?”\n\n“It would be stable.”\n\n“That is not what I asked.”\n\n“It would be good for my future.”\n\n“That is also not what I asked.”\n\nI turned the drumstick in my hand. “I don’t know how to want something without proving it makes sense.”\n\nMara's expression softened. “Then start with whether you want to wake up there.”\n\nThe question stayed with me all the way home.\n\nThat evening, I found Greg in the van, replacing one of the moon-shaped hooks. He looked up when I climbed inside.\n\n“The hook is not broken,” I said.\n\n“It was emotionally loose.”\n\nI sat on the bed. “I got the email.”\n\n“The answer is due tomorrow.”\n\n“Yes.”\n\nGreg put down the screwdriver.\n\n“I think I want to take it,” I said.\n\nHe inhaled slowly. “Okay.”\n\n“You’re not going to say anything?”\n\n“What do you want me to say?”\n\n“I don’t know.”\n\n“There’s that word again.”\n\nI looked toward the open doors. “I want you to ask me to stay.”\n\nGreg's face changed.\n\n“I want you to tell me I’m making a mistake,” I continued. “I want you to make it impossible for me to leave so I can blame you if I do.”\n\nHe moved closer. “I won’t.”\n\n“I know.”\n\n“I love you.”\n\n“I know.”\n\n“I want you here.”\n\n“I know.”\n\n“But I don’t want to become another person who decides that my fear is more important than your freedom.”\n\nThe tears came before I could stop them.\n\nGreg crouched in front of me. “Can I touch you?”\n\nI nodded.\n\nHe took my hands. “If you go, I will miss you. I will be scared. I will probably write an extremely embarrassing song about highway exits.”\n\n“That sounds terrible.”\n\n“It will be.”\n\n“You might not wait.”\n\n“I can’t promise I’ll stay exactly the same. I can promise I won’t pretend I’m fine if I’m not.”\n\n“And if I stay?”\n\n“Then I’ll still ask what you want next month. You don’t have to turn staying into a lifetime sentence.”\n\nI looked at the space I had made in the van: my books, my scarf, the metal pick.\n\n“I’m afraid that if I stay, I’m choosing you instead of myself.”\n\n“Then don’t choose me,” Greg said. “Choose the life that lets you be yourself. If I’m in it, I’ll be grateful. If I’m not, I’ll survive.”\n\nThe kindness felt like a blade.\n\nI leaned forward and pressed my forehead to his.\n\n“I don’t want to go,” I whispered.\n\nGreg closed his eyes.\n\n“I want the job because it’s good,” I continued. “But I want to wake up here. I want the bakery noise. I want the community center. I want your terrible songs.”\n\n“They’re not all terrible.”\n\n“Most.”\n\n“That’s fair.”\n\n“I want you.”\n\nHis hands tightened around mine. “Are you sure?”\n\n“Yes.”\n\n“You can still change your mind.”\n\n“I know.”\n\nHe kissed me. The van was quiet except for the ocean and the soft creak of the mattress.\n\nThe kiss became warmer. Greg asked. I answered. He moved slowly, giving me every chance to stop, and I held him closer instead.\n\nThe doors closed. The email waited, unanswered, while the two of us chose the present with open eyes.\n\nIn the morning, I declined the job.\n\nNot because Greg had asked me to stay.\n\nBecause I had finally asked myself where I wanted to wake up.\n\nThe answer was Beach City.\n\nFor now.\n\nThe next morning, I went to the community center and told Mara I had declined the position.\n\nShe listened while arranging a box of percussion instruments.\n\n“Do you regret it?” she asked.\n\n“Not today.”\n\n“That is the answer you have.”\n\n“I’m trying to stop demanding answers from the future.”\n\nMara handed me a tambourine. “A sensible policy.”\n\nGreg arrived at noon with pastries and a paper map.\n\n“I have a proposal,” he announced.\n\n“Is it about a road trip?”\n\n“It is about several road trips, one festival, and an emergency pastry reserve.”\n\n“You are responding to my career decision with baked goods.”\n\n“I am responding with joy.”\n\nHe spread the map across the table. There were stars drawn beside towns, beaches, and music halls.\n\n“You don’t have to plan my life,” I said.\n\n“I’m planning experiences. You can reject all of them.”\n\n“Why are you doing this?”\n\n“Because I want a future with you that has more than one shape.”\n\nThe words made me quiet.\n\nGreg tapped one of the stars. “You can work here. You can travel. You can change your mind. We can be together without making your whole life fit inside my van.”\n\n“That is a very good plan.”\n\n“I have been practicing.”\n\nI folded the map. “I choose the lake.”\n\nHis smile appeared immediately. “The lake it is.”\n\n“And the festival.”\n\n“Both?”\n\n“I contain multitudes.”\n\nHe kissed me in the empty hallway.\n\nThe job offer did not vanish. It remained in my email, a reminder that opportunity could exist without being accepted. Beach City was not the only possible future.\n\nIt was the one I wanted now.\n\nThat distinction made staying feel like freedom instead of fear.\n\nThat night, I wrote my own list. Reasons to stay. Reasons to travel. Things I wanted independent of Greg. Things I wanted with him.\n\nThe list was messy. Some reasons contradicted each other. I wanted roots and roads. Stability and surprise. A room of my own and a place beside his.\n\nGreg read it only when I offered.\n\n“This is a good life,” he said.\n\n“It is a complicated one.”\n\n“Those are not opposites.”\n\nI folded the paper. “I am not choosing you instead of myself.”\n\n“I know.”\n\n“I am choosing a self that includes you.”\n\nHis eyes softened. “That is the best kind of choice.”\n\nI put the list on the refrigerator. Greg added a magnet shaped like a pancake.\n\n“This is not a serious household system,” I said.\n\n“It is a flexible household system.”\n\n“You are hiding wisdom behind breakfast.”\n\n“Breakfast has carried me through many difficult decisions.”\n\nI kissed him in the kitchen. The future remained complicated, but it was no longer a single road with Greg standing at the end of it.\n\nIt was a map I could both hold.\n\nI placed it beneath the pancake magnet. Greg stood behind me with his arms around my waist.\n\n“I like this,” he said.\n\n“The map?”\n\n“The way you stay without disappearing.”\n\nI turned and kissed him.\n\nThe pancake magnet held the map in place.\n\nIt stayed there through breakfast, through the afternoon, and through another quiet conversation about what I wanted next.\n\nWe both listened.\n\nThe map stayed.","word_count":1809},"su-32":{"number":32,"title":"The Work of Staying","subtitle":"Stars Between the Strings · Chapter 32 of 40","content":"The application had been sitting under the car wash receipts for two days.\n\nGreg wasn't snooping. He was looking for the electricity bill, which was somehow more embarrassing. He lifted the envelope, saw my name, and put it back on the table without opening it.\n\n\"That looks important,\" he said.\n\n\"It is.\"\n\n\"Want to tell me?\"\n\nI looked at the plastic chair across from him. I wanted to say it wasn't decided, which was true, and leave the rest until I knew what I wanted. But he would notice me being careful with every word.\n\n\"I got offered a job,\" I said. \"Not here.\"\n\nHe smiled immediately. \"That's fantastic.\"\n\n\"It's across the water. I'd have to spend longer stretches away from Beach City.\"\n\n\"Still fantastic.\"\n\nThe fluorescent light buzzed above us. I watched him straighten a stack of receipts that didn't need straightening.\n\n\"Greg.\"\n\n\"What?\"\n\n\"You can be happy for me and still have feelings about the distance.\"\n\nHe stopped moving. \"I know.\"\n\n\"Then why are you pretending this is as easy as finding a new parking spot?\"\n\nHe looked down. \"Because I was afraid if I said I'd miss you, it'd sound like I was asking you to give it up.\"\n\nThere it was. The thing beneath the joke. I pulled out the other chair.\n\n\"Sit?\"\n\nHe sat. The electricity bill fell from his hand and landed upside down between us.\n\n\"I'm not asking permission,\" I said. \"I am asking whether you want to talk about what this would mean for us.\"\n\n\"Yeah. I do.\"\n\nWe started with the stupidly practical parts because they were the parts we could actually do something about. The job paid enough to cover travel if I didn't spend it all getting back and forth for one-night visits. Greg had bookings on certain weekends, including a school fundraiser he'd already promised to play. I had other partners and commitments beyond Beach City; none of those disappeared because a job offer existed.\n\n\"I know you've been clear about being ethically non-monogamous,\" he said. \"And that you prefer polyamory. I guess I keep thinking a schedule's gonna make me sound like I'm booking a slot in your life.\"\n\n\"You are in my life. A schedule just means I don't promise to be in two places on the same Thursday.\"\n\nHe laughed softly. \"That'd make a good song.\"\n\n\"Only if you leave the receipts out of it.\"\n\nWe wrote down what we could promise. We would tell each other about changes early enough to matter. Neither of us would treat silence as a punishment. We could say we wanted more time without using guilt to get it. Visits would be invitations, not surprise tests. If either of us couldn't make something work, we would talk about it instead of announcing a failure.\n\nWhen we finished the first page of our plan, I went to the board by the sink and wrote down the dates I'd have to answer the job offer. I needed an actual deadline, not one I could move whenever the decision made me nervous.\n\nGreg looked at it. \"So there's an interview?\"\n\n\"Yes. A second one. I didn't tell you because I didn't know whether I wanted to go.\"\n\n\"Do you?\"\n\n\"I want to find out what the work is like when nobody's trying to impress me.\"\n\nHe thought about that, then nodded.\n\nWe made a list of questions I could ask without surrendering the chance to accept. Would the job pay for travel when I needed to cross the water? Who approved a schedule change? Would I be expected to take on duties that belonged to people already working there? If I chose to leave after the trial period, would I still be able to come back without being treated as a traitor?\n\nGreg laughed at the last one. \"That sounds like a lot of your life.\"\n\n\"Also sounds like I should've checked the bus timetable before offering to drive you every weekend,\" Greg said.\n\nHe pulled a car-wash receipt across the desk and did sums on the back. Fuel, the van's increasingly unreliable starter, his own shifts and the days Steven might need him. No grand promises. Just ugly arithmetic.\n\nHe tried adding the numbers again, as though a different pencil mark might persuade the van to run on good intentions.\n\n“Okay,” he said. “Problem. If I drive out Friday night and come back Sunday, I miss the Saturday morning wash shift. I can ask Mara to swap, but she has a life, so I should probably ask before I draw an entire love story on a receipt.”\n\n“That's growth.”\n\n“Don't tell Steven. He'll think I'm becoming responsible.”\n\nI was laughing when his phone buzzed. It was a message from Steven asking whether Greg could help move a box of things out of the old storage room over the weekend.\n\nGreg showed it to me, then frowned at his penciled timetable.\n\n“See? There it is. Something I actually said I'd do.”\n\n“You can tell him you're busy.”\n\n“I can, but I already told him yes. And I want to be there.” He looked embarrassed. “He's grown up, but that doesn't mean I'm done being his dad.”\n\n“I don't want you to be.”\n\nGreg made a face at the numbers, tore off the first version and began again. In the new plan he had space to help Steven, work his shift and visit me another day if my interview went well. The travel estimate wasn't especially romantic. It also didn't require anyone to break a promise.\n\nWhen he finished, he turned the receipt over to hide the calculations. On the blank side he drew a tiny van with a heart on its roof.\n\n“Patent pending,” he said. “The Universe Mobile. Runs on affection and questionable mechanics.”\n\n“That last part I believe.”\n\nHe gave me a look of theatrical betrayal. I leaned across the desk to kiss him, and the paper slid off the table between us. We both reached for it at once and knocked our heads together.\n\n“See?” he groaned. “Terrible engineering.”\n\nI kissed his forehead this time. “Excellent research data.”\n\n\"Two trips, maybe three,\" he said. \"More if the van figures out how to become a boat.\"\n\n\"I didn't ask you to be my ferry.\"\n\n\"Yeah. I know.\" He tapped four fingers on the paper, paused, and tried a different rhythm. \"I do this thing where I volunteer before I check whether I can show up.\"\n\nHe grinned. \"I had a whole speech planned. Super supportive boyfriend, excellent hair, totally cool about distance.\"\n\n\"Your hair wasn't part of the interview.\"\n\n\"Well, it should be.\"\n\nHis grin faded.\n\n\"I don't want you to turn it down,\" he said. \"I also don't want to wave goodbye and spend a month hearing every missed call as a sad song.\"\n\n\"Then don't write the song alone.\"\n\n\"Oh, I started it. It's terrible.\" He pushed the receipt toward me. \"This is better. Boring numbers, real phone calls, me actually showing up.\"\n\nI wrote the interview date beside his fuel estimate, leaving room to change both. Greg returned the electricity bill to the top of his stack; that was what he'd been looking for in the first place.\n\n\"It is.\"\n\nHe stood, opened the little office window, and let in air that smelled of soap and summer asphalt. The car wash machinery had stopped for the day. The silence made the room feel like someplace we could choose to be.\n\n\"Can I tell you what scares me?\" he asked.\n\n\"Yes.\"\n\n\"Not that you'll like the job. I hope you do. I think I'm scared I'll start treating your coming home like proof you still love me, and then every delayed bus will make me a jerk.\"\n\nI leaned against the desk. \"Then we have to notice when that starts happening. I can't guarantee I won't miss a visit. I can guarantee I won't make you guess whether I mean to come.\"\n\nHe looked toward the calendar.\n\n\"Could we plan the first week after the interview before either of us promises the whole year?\"\n\n\"That sounds perfect.\"\n\n\"Wow. You said perfect. Write that down.\"\n\nI did, in the margin of the page he was holding. He tried not to smile and failed.\n\nOur non-exclusive relationship wasn't a test of how much distance we could endure. It was two people trying not to let the fear of a future decision take the place of one we could make today.\n\nI asked whether he wanted to walk to the diner before we returned to the application. He did. He held out his hand, and when I took it he looked as pleased as he had the first time we played a song together.\n\nGreg pointed at the page. \"This feels very grown-up.\"\n\n\"Terrifying, isn't it?\"\n\n\"I was hoping love came with fewer columns.\"\n\nI nudged his knee with mine. \"You have a business. You know columns.\"\n\n\"That's different. The columns at the car wash don't have feelings.\"\n\n\"Your accounts would suggest otherwise.\"\n\nHe laughed, then went quiet.\n\n\"I do want you to stay,\" he said. \"I know I don't get to decide for you. But I want you to know that.\"\n\nThe admission made my throat tighten. There was something braver about hearing his preference without having to fight for my freedom first.\n\n\"Thank you,\" I said. \"I haven't decided.\"\n\n\"Okay.\"\n\n\"And if I take the job, I still want to come home here sometimes. Not because I owe you proof. Because I like this stupid office and your terrible coffee.\"\n\n\"Hey. The coffee is improving.\"\n\n\"It's learning to apologize.\"\n\nHe reached across the table. \"Can I hold your hand?\"\n\n\"Yes.\"\n\nWe sat with our fingers tangled between the bill and the job application. He didn't ask to see the letter. I didn't hand it over to prove I trusted him.\n\nAfter closing, we walked to the van. The air had turned cool. A light flickered in the car wash sign, and he made a note to call someone about it before the next wet morning.\n\nInside the van, he put on a quiet recording—not one of the old songs that felt too loaded, but a rough instrumental he'd made the previous week. I leaned against his shoulder.\n\n\"Stay for dinner?\" he asked.\n\n\"Yes. Tonight.\"\n\n\"Tonight's pretty good.\"\n\nHe kissed me when I turned toward him. We had all the time we wanted for that moment, and no right to assume anything about the next one. Later, we went inside and let the night become ours.\n\nIn the morning, the application was still unanswered. I put it in the drawer beside my toothbrush. We would talk again when I knew more, not pretend a single night could settle a whole future.","word_count":1791},"su-42":{"number":42,"title":"Folding Chairs and Honest Labor","subtitle":"The Lanternweald — expansion pass two","content":"Greg had agreed to perform at a fundraiser before anyone told him there was no one assigned to put away the chairs.\n\nThat was how I found him at Starwake Pier, holding a box of tangled lantern cords in one hand and a folding table under his other arm.\n\n\"How's the glamorous life of a musician?\" I asked.\n\n\"Nobody warned me about furniture.\"\n\n\"Did you read the volunteer sheet?\"\n\n\"Yep.\"\n\n\"Did you put your own name next to every empty task?\"\n\nGreg glanced at the paper pinned to a post. Nearly every line had GREG written beside it.\n\n\"That is a wildly unfair accusation,\" he said. \"Some of those say 'Greg U.'\"\n\nHe was trying to make me laugh, which was ordinarily a successful strategy. I took the table from him instead.\n\n\"You're performing tonight. You also have sound checks, and you're supposed to make sure the speakers don't blow the fuse again. You can't do everything.\"\n\nGreg lowered the tangled lantern cords to the boards as if they might bite him.\n\n\"Okay. New plan. I play music, and someone qualified handles the part where nobody gets electrocuted.\"\n\nTamsin looked up from her inspection sheet. \"That's my part. And I need the proper cable, not an enthusiastic volunteer with a screwdriver.\"\n\n\"Music to my ears,\" Greg said. \"Well, technically that's music to my electrician.\"\n\nShe didn't laugh. She did point out the stage's unmarked access route, so we moved two speaker stands before anyone tripped over them.\n\nI crossed three names off Greg's volunteer assignments and asked Rowan about taking the first sound check. Rowan named a fee. Greg agreed to it without turning paid work into a touching story about community spirit.\n\n\"I'm in danger of becoming organized,\" he whispered.\n\n\"You still haven't found your capo.\"\n\nHe held up a clothespin. \"Technology is what you make of it.\"\n\nBy dusk, the equipment inventory had two signatures and the pay sheet listed actual hours. Greg brought a small tin of Iroh's citrus-and-smoke tea to the booth, the same batch he'd tasted at the Jasmine Dragon. I watched him read the brewing note twice and follow it anyway.\n\nOne sip made him wrinkle his nose. He took another.\n\n\"Your tea guy knows what he's doing,\" he said. \"Still tastes like a campfire got a music scholarship.\"\n\n\"Are you going to tell him that?\"\n\n\"Oh, absolutely. Then I'm gonna ask if he wants to hear my terrible guitar joke.\"\n\nI kissed him quickly, before the stage lights came up. He brushed his thumb over my hand and reached for his guitar, ready to be Greg and nobody else's version of wise.\n\n\"I know.\"\n\n\"You don't sound convinced.\"\n\nGreg set the cords down.\n\n\"People need the money. The pier needs new supports. If somebody says they'll do a job and then doesn't, I can at least make sure it happens.\"\n\n\"I know why you do it. That doesn't make the plan workable.\"\n\nHe rubbed his face. The sun caught on the edges of his hair.\n\n\"Do you have a better plan?\"\n\n\"I was hoping we could make one together.\"\n\nWe began with the chairs. There were forty-eight of them, three with loose hinges and one whose left leg did not quite touch the ground. I wrote the number at the top of the volunteer sheet and set the broken ones aside.\n\nGreg inspected the crooked chair.\n\n\"Hey, this one's got character.\"\n\n\"This one has a lawsuit.\"\n\nHe laughed.\n\nThe fundraiser was meant to pay for safer pier railings and a storage shed so equipment wouldn't sit exposed to saltwater all winter. Greg had offered music. Mara had offered food. Tamsin had offered to oversee the repairs. Somewhere between the promises, nobody had agreed who would collect donations or clean up afterward.\n\nI borrowed a pencil from Pip and crossed GREG off the tasks he hadn't been asked to own.\n\n\"Somebody still has to do those,\" he said.\n\n\"Yes. Somebody can volunteer.\"\n\n\"What if they don't?\"\n\n\"Then we scale down the event. We don't pretend you owe free labor to everyone because you know how to play a guitar.\"\n\nHe sat on the edge of the stage. His expression softened, then tightened.\n\n\"Sometimes it feels like if I say no, the whole thing falls apart.\"\n\n\"Then the thing was badly designed.\"\n\nHe looked at me.\n\n\"That's a weirdly comforting insult to an event.\"\n\n\"One of my specialties.\"\n\nWe spent an hour asking people what they could actually do. Katara said her clinic volunteers could help with refreshments for an hour, not all night. Danse agreed to check the temporary stage railings but would not sign off on the damaged western section. Iroh could supervise tea and cleanup until dusk; after that, he had an appointment of his own.\n\nNola arrived for the sound check carrying a case of cables and a schedule that looked significantly less forgiving than Greg's hopeful volunteer sheet.\n\n\"Which of these jobs is paid?\" she asked.\n\nGreg read the paper. \"Uh. None of them?\"\n\n\"Then my crew's only doing the work in the contract. We have another booking tomorrow morning.\"\n\nShe wasn't being cruel. She was watching two sound technicians haul expensive equipment across a damp pier while half the volunteers argued about a banner.\n\nGreg scratched his beard. \"I was going to do the teardown myself.\"\n\n\"You can't disconnect a stage you're still playing on,\" Nola said.\n\n\"Well, when you put it that way.\"\n\nMaui appeared carrying the marked stack of chairs. \"Fear not! The great Maui can clear the entire pier with one hand.\"\n\n\"Please use both,\" Tamsin called from the railing. \"And don't stack them in the exit.\"\n\nMaui looked as though somebody had insulted the artistry of his shoulders. Greg grinned and tried a little drumroll on a chair seat. The chair squealed.\n\n\"Don't give him percussion,\" I said.\n\nNola checked the booking sheet. She moved the sound test earlier and made a separate line for the load-out crew. Danse could check the approved stage railings, but the electrical load would still have to be signed off by the responsible technician.\n\nGreg listened to the new timing. \"So two sets, no mystery third set, and I don't become a chair company afterward.\"\n\n\"Correct,\" Nola said.\n\nHe hummed a rising four-note phrase. It sounded like relief.\n\nMaui stopped beside us. \"Where's my name on the program?\"\n\n\"Under chairs,\" I told him.\n\nHe sighed. \"You people refuse to recognize an artist.\"\n\nMaui offered to carry every chair personally.\n\nI handed him the stack labeled FIFTEEN CHAIRS, TWO PEOPLE.\n\nHe read it.\n\n\"You're limiting my heroism.\"\n\n\"I'm improving its scheduling.\"\n\nThe sound check introduced a new problem.\n\nGreg's amplifier buzzed when the stage crew switched on the lantern string. Danse watched the voltage meter and asked to shut the shared circuit down until they could identify the load.\n\n“Hey, no argument here,” Greg said. “I've already had one amp go out with a pop. Not doing that on purpose.”\n\nMaui appeared beside them with a coil of cable balanced on his hook. “I can hang the lanterns from the tallest post. Boom. Problem solved.”\n\n“Problem relocated,” Danse corrected. “The post hasn't been inspected for that load.”\n\nMaui looked offended. “I've lifted islands.”\n\n“And we appreciate the resume,” Greg said. “But the island probably didn't have a building inspector.”\n\nI laughed; Maui did not. He put the cable down and asked how he could help without making the power problem worse. Nola directed him to the approved string of unlit lanterns at the back of the square. He carried them with the grave solemnity of someone demonstrating world-shaking restraint.\n\nGreg checked the amplifier's cable, turned the gain down, and waited while the qualified technician tested a separate circuit. The replacement power lead didn't reach as far as the original, so he agreed to a smaller stage area and changed the set list to suit the room.\n\n“You okay with being less loud?” I asked him.\n\n“Less loud?” he said, aghast. “Jasper, I can make an acoustic guitar sound embarrassing all by itself.”\n\nDanse recorded the permitted load. Maui came back carrying the folding chairs he'd been assigned. They both looked pleased with work that hadn't required a dramatic announcement.\n\nIt wasn't a contest over who could save the concert. People had come to hear music and raise money for repairs. We were trying not to give them another repair bill.\n\nAt noon we had six volunteers, a safe seating plan, and a stage that did not require anyone to stand on the broken section. Greg looked at the sheet, then at me.\n\n\"You know the terrible thing?\"\n\n\"What?\"\n\n\"It works.\"\n\n\"I do hate it when that happens.\"\n\nThe first guests arrived while Greg tuned his guitar. Some came from Beach City; others wandered over from the Lanternwell Square market. A child asked whether all the songs would be about doors. Greg promised at least one about chairs, which earned me a look from Mara that could have curdled milk.\n\nA man wearing a wide brown hat stopped beside the stage.\n\n\"You're Greg Universe, right?\"\n\n\"That's me.\"\n\n\"Great! Heard you were playing all night. Can you do another three songs after the fundraiser? My kid's a huge fan.\"\n\nGreg smiled out of reflex.\n\n\"Uh, maybe—\"\n\nI watched the word forming.\n\nThen he stopped.\n\n\"Actually,\" he said, \"I've agreed to two sets. I can sign the kid's program afterward if they want, but I've got to help with the equipment before heading home.\"\n\nThe man blinked.\n\n\"You're not doing an encore?\"\n\n\"Not tonight.\"\n\nFor a terrible second, I thought the man might argue. Instead, he shrugged and went to find seats.\n\nGreg turned toward me, looking almost surprised.\n\n\"That was uncomfortable.\"\n\n\"And?\"\n\n\"And the sky did not fall.\"\n\n\"Good. We're running low on replacement sky.\"\n\nHe laughed, then reached for my hand. His fingers were rough against my palm from the guitar strings.\n\n\"You okay with me learning this slowly?\" he asked.\n\n\"I'd be suspicious if you learned it all at once.\"\n\nThe concert went well enough to feel like a real concert instead of a miracle. One microphone buzzed. The first song began a little late because a child dropped a cup in the front row. The audience sang the chorus of Greg's old road song louder than he did.\n\nHalfway through the second set, a light on the pier flickered.\n\nDanse stood at once.\n\nGreg stopped playing.\n\n\"Problem?\"\n\n\"Power fluctuation. Keep everyone off the western platform.\"\n\nThere was no panic. We had actually rehearsed what to do.\n\nMaui helped move chairs to the safer end. Katara brought the children to the square. Greg put down his guitar and checked the speaker plugs while Danse tested the line. They found a loose connection and decided not to restart the stage circuit.\n\nThe remaining songs would be acoustic.\n\n\"I can do acoustic,\" Greg announced to the audience. \"It's mostly what I do anyway.\"\n\nSomeone cheered.\n\nThe night finished with a small circle of people and no dramatic lighting. I preferred it. Greg's voice carried across the water, catching in the warm space between the lanterns. I could see how tired he was, and how happy he was that he hadn't spent the whole evening trying to be four people at once.\n\nWhen the final note faded, he looked at me over the strings.\n\nI smiled.\n\nHe smiled back.\n\nThen he stood up and helped fold the first chair.\n\n\"You're not allowed to do all of them,\" I reminded him.\n\n\"Just this one.\"\n\n\"Fine.\"\n\nWe stacked them in pairs, carried equipment to the dry storeroom, and counted the donation box with Mara present. The fundraiser would cover the railings, not the new shed. That was less than we'd hoped. It was enough to begin.\n\nThe last guest left with a paper cup of tea and the promise that the next event would have a proper cleanup roster.\n\nGreg and I walked toward the Starwake Door after midnight.\n\nHe was carrying only his guitar. I was carrying the repaired volunteer sheet, folded small enough to fit in my pocket.\n\n\"Good night?\" I asked.\n\n\"Best one I've had in a while.\"\n\n\"Even without an encore?\"\n\n\"Especially without one.\"\n\nHe tucked a loose strand of hair behind my ear. The touch was gentle, a question rather than a claim.\n\n\"Can I give you one private encore?\"\n\n\"Depends. Is it about chairs?\"\n\n\"No promises.\"\n\nI kissed him before he could invent a second verse.\n\nLater, when the van door closed behind us, we left the music and the rest of the evening to ourselves.\n\nIn the morning, Greg found the volunteer sheet on the dashboard. Under my handwriting he'd added a new rule:\n\nGREG IS ALLOWED TO BE A MUSICIAN, NOT A FURNITURE COMPANY.\n\nI added one more line beneath it.\n\nJASPER IS NOT THE ENTIRE MANAGEMENT DEPARTMENT EITHER.\n\nHe kissed my cheek while I wrote.\n\nWe both liked the amended version.","word_count":2155},"su-48":{"number":48,"title":"An Instrument Returned","subtitle":"The Lanternweald — fifth expansion pass","content":"The guitar came back in a cardboard box.\n\nThat was never a good sign.\n\nGreg stood on the steps of Starwake Pier with the box in his hands and an expression that didn't quite know whether to laugh or cry. The instrument belonged to him. He'd lent it to a young musician named Rowan, who had promised to bring it back after the Listening Fair.\n\nRowan stood nearby, twisting their fingers together.\n\n\"I'm really sorry,\" they said.\n\nGreg opened the box.\n\nThe neck was intact, but the bridge had lifted from the body. One string was broken, and a long scratch ran along the lower edge where the finish had been scraped away.\n\nI felt my stomach drop.\n\n\"Oh, Greg.\"\n\nHe looked at the instrument for a long time.\n\nThen he put it down.\n\n\"What happened?\" he asked.\n\nRowan swallowed.\n\n\"The stand tipped when somebody moved a crate. I caught it before it hit the floor, but the case slipped. I thought I could fix the string, and then I saw the bridge.\"\n\n\"Did anyone get hurt?\"\n\n\"No.\"\n\nGreg breathed out.\n\n\"Okay. That's something.\"\n\nRowan looked startled.\n\n\"You're not angry?\"\n\n\"I'm disappointed. It's an old guitar. I wanted it back in one piece.\"\n\n\"I can pay for the repair.\"\n\n\"Let's find out what it needs before we promise anything.\"\n\nI looked at him. He was upset, and he wasn't hiding it under a joke. That was new enough to make me proud.\n\nWe took the guitar to Brasswatch Open Workshop.\n\nDanse was running a training session on careful measuring. Renn had a row of small metal fittings laid out on the bench. When Greg arrived carrying the box, everyone paused.\n\n\"Musical instrument,\" Danse said.\n\n\"Broken musical instrument,\" Greg corrected.\n\nDanse examined the bridge.\n\n\"The adhesive has failed.\"\n\n\"Yes, but the wood might still be sound,\" Greg said.\n\nDanse reached for a tool.\n\nI touched his sleeve.\n\n\"Ask first.\"\n\nHe stopped.\n\n\"May I examine it?\"\n\nGreg nodded.\n\nThe first ten minutes went badly.\n\nDanse wanted to measure every piece before deciding on a repair. Greg knew which parts of the guitar had always creaked and which changes would affect its sound. Both were right about something. Both kept interrupting the other.\n\n\"If you clamp it there, you'll change the resonance,\" Greg said.\n\n\"If I don't stabilize it, the bridge will lift again.\"\n\n\"I know, but—\"\n\n\"You haven't let me finish.\"\n\n\"Neither have you.\"\n\nI raised a hand.\n\n\"Would either of you like to hear the other person's sentence?\"\n\nThey looked at me.\n\nRenn laughed.\n\n\"That seems fair.\"\n\nWe tried again.\n\nGreg explained how the instrument sounded before the damage and which imperfections were part of its history. Danse described the structural repair needed to make it safe to restring. Renn took notes and asked whether they could handle the small fittings under supervision.\n\nGreg took the guitar out of the box and showed Danse the shallow scratch near the lower edge. It was old, smooth to the touch, and nowhere near the broken bridge.\n\n“Leave that,” he said.\n\nDanse inspected it. “It doesn't affect the structure.”\n\n“Yeah. I know.”\n\n“Then it won't be included in the repair.”\n\nGreg looked at him for a moment, surprised that he hadn't needed to explain why a mark could matter without being damage. Then he pointed to the bridge.\n\n“This one is new. Listen when I pluck the low string. Hear that little rattle?”\n\nDanse leaned closer, listening with the concentration he usually gave a suspicious engine. “Yes. The bridge isn't seated.”\n\n“Right. And if you glue it down wrong, it'll hold but sound awful. Kind of like building a truck that drives but only in a straight line.”\n\nDanse considered the analogy. “A badly aligned truck would still be dangerous.”\n\n“Man, you really know how to make a metaphor file a safety report.”\n\nRenn laughed from the other bench. Danse gave them a look that would have silenced most Brotherhood recruits. It didn't work on Greg, who was already tapping a rhythm on the body of the guitar.\n\n“I can test the tone,” Greg offered. “You do the structural bit. We both know our end of this thing.”\n\nDanse set down the measuring tool. “Agreed. But you wait for me to certify the joint before you put tension on it.”\n\n“Deal.”\n\nThere was a little pause. Then Danse reached for Greg's hand, not to shake it but to look at the calluses across his fingers.\n\n“You play frequently?”\n\n“Every chance I get.”\n\nDanse turned the hand over and released it. “Then you should have an instrument that won't injure you.”\n\nGreg's grin softened. “Yeah. I'd like that.”\n\nBy afternoon they'd agreed on a plan.\n\nThe bridge could be reattached after the old glue was cleaned away. The damaged finish didn't need to be made invisible. The new string would be matched to the rest of the set. Greg would test the instrument before the repair was considered complete.\n\nRowan arrived after the inspection.\n\n\"How much?\" they asked.\n\nDanse showed them the estimate.\n\nRowan looked relieved.\n\n\"I can afford that if I pay in two parts.\"\n\nGreg nodded.\n\n\"Then let's do that.\"\n\nHe hesitated.\n\n\"Would you still lend me a guitar sometime?\"\n\nGreg looked at the box.\n\n\"Not this one for a while.\"\n\nRowan's face fell.\n\n\"But if you're still learning, I can help you find an instrument you can practice on. One that doesn't cost more than your rent.\"\n\nRowan smiled.\n\n\"Thank you.\"\n\nI watched Greg carefully. It would have been easy for him to pretend the damage didn't matter because he wanted to be generous. Instead he'd asked for a repair and left room for a relationship that might become different.\n\nBefore the repair began, Rowan returned with a small envelope. They had counted out the first payment and wanted to know whether the rest could be paid after their next shift.\n\nGreg looked at Danse. Danse looked at the estimate.\n\n\"That is what the agreement says,\" Danse answered. \"We don't alter a signed repayment plan because someone seems anxious.\"\n\nRowan relaxed a little. I could see that they'd expected the adults in the room to be angry enough to demand everything at once.\n\n\"Can I help with the repair?\" they asked.\n\n\"Not on this instrument,\" Greg said gently. \"I want the workshop to do it. But you can watch from the marked area, if Danse says it's safe.\"\n\nDanse set out a clean tray, explained the hot tools and the safe path to the exit. He did not ask Rowan to handle expensive equipment in exchange for the privilege of being there. Renn took the scheduled shop shift; everyone whose time was being used would be paid according to their agreement.\n\nThe repair took longer than Greg wanted. He kept hovering in the doorway, then walking away, then returning with a new question he probably knew the answer to already.\n\nI found him by the water pump.\n\n\"You can be upset,\" I said.\n\n\"I know. I don't want to yell at the kid.\"\n\n\"Being upset doesn't mean you're going to.\"\n\nHe looked at his hands. \"That guitar was with me when I didn't have much else.\"\n\nI remembered the storage room, the old posters and the letter he had chosen not to destroy. The scratch on a piece of wood wasn't the most painful part of the repair; it was having to trust somebody else with a thing that had once helped him survive.\n\n\"Do you need me to stay here?\" I asked.\n\n\"If you've got time.\"\n\n\"I do. I can sit with you, or I can help Renn sort the inventory. Your choice.\"\n\nHe smiled, half embarrassed. \"Could you just sit?\"\n\nI sat beside him on the low wall. We watched the workshop door open and close while people came through with their own ordinary problems. He didn't have to turn the mistake into a forgiveness speech. Rowan didn't have to become his protégé to make the repayment acceptable.\n\nWhen the first test note sounded from inside, Greg stood so quickly I nearly lost my balance.\n\nDanse appeared holding the guitar.\n\n\"Not finished,\" he said. \"But the joint is holding.\"\n\nGreg let out a breath, laughed at himself, then reached for my hand. I squeezed back.\n\nAfter Rowan left, Danse cleaned the remaining glue residue from the bridge's outer edge before the final finish work. Greg watched with folded arms.\n\n\"You're hovering,\" Danse said.\n\n\"That's my guitar.\"\n\n\"Yes.\"\n\n\"An important guitar.\"\n\n\"I understand.\"\n\nGreg looked surprised.\n\nDanse set the tool down.\n\n\"I once owned equipment that held my whole sense of identity. I know it can be difficult to let someone else repair it.\"\n\nGreg smiled.\n\n\"Yeah. Something like that.\"\n\nThey worked together until evening.\n\nWhen the bridge had set enough for the next stage, Danse placed the guitar in a safe rack and labeled it with the date. Greg signed the work order and left his contact information for the final test.\n\nOutside, the sky was turning pink.\n\n\"How do you feel?\" I asked.\n\nGreg looked at the workshop door.\n\n\"Still disappointed.\"\n\n\"That's allowed.\"\n\n\"And a little better.\"\n\n\"Also allowed.\"\n\nHe took my hand.\n\n\"I used to think if somebody broke something I loved, I had to pretend it was fine or they'd think I didn't love them.\"\n\n\"Now?\"\n\n\"Now I think I can tell them it hurt without turning them into a villain.\"\n\nI smiled.\n\nHe leaned close and kissed me, warm and familiar. The smell of salt and wood glue clung to his jacket.\n\n\"Want to get dinner?\" he asked.\n\n\"Yes.\"\n\n\"Somewhere with chairs that aren't broken?\"\n\n\"That seems ambitious.\"\n\nHe laughed.\n\nTwo days later, we returned for the sound test. The guitar played clearly, though the repaired bridge made one low note sound slightly warmer than before.\n\nGreg listened.\n\nThen he played the same little melody again.\n\n\"Huh.\"\n\n\"Is that bad?\" Danse asked.\n\nGreg smiled.\n\n\"No. It's different.\"\n\nHe looked at the scratch along the lower edge.\n\n\"I think I'll leave that part.\"\n\nDanse nodded.\n\n\"Your decision.\"\n\nGreg tucked the guitar into its case.\n\n\"Thanks.\"\n\nThey shook hands.\n\nFor once, neither man seemed to need to prove he had been the most useful person in the room.","word_count":1700}};var s=lib.series.find(x=>x.series_slug==="stars-between-the-strings");if(!s)return;for(var i=0;i<s.chapters.length;i++){var c=s.chapters[i],e=edits[c.id];if(e)Object.assign(c,e);}s.total_word_count=82453;})();
